                        @* Section 5 — MT4 *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s5">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">5. Position Size Calculator for MT4</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">MetaTrader 4 does not include a built-in position size calculator in the trade ticket, but there are several straightforward ways to calculate lot sizes directly on the platform.</p>
                            <div class="br-platform-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;">
                                <div class="br-platform-card">
                                    <div class="br-platform-icon" style="color:#2563eb;background:#eff6ff;"><i class="fa-solid fa-plug"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-name">MT4 Position Size Indicator / EA</div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-desc">Download a free position size calculator indicator from the MQL4 Market. Once installed, it appears on your chart — input your risk % and drag the SL line and it displays the correct lot size in real time. Popular option: "Position Size Calculator" by EarnForex.</div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-tags">
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">Free</span>
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">Chart Overlay</span>
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">Real-Time</span>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-platform-card">
                                    <div class="br-platform-icon" style="color:#16a34a;background:#f0fdf4;"><i class="fa-solid fa-file-invoice-dollar"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-name">MT4 Trade Ticket — Manual Entry</div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-desc">Calculate position size using the formula externally, then type the lot size into the "Volume" field on the MT4 New Order ticket. Most common approach for discretionary traders who calculate once before entering.</div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-tags">
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">Manual</span>
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">No Install</span>
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">Full Control</span>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="br-platform-card">
                                    <div class="br-platform-icon" style="color:#7c3aed;background:#f5f3ff;"><i class="fa-solid fa-robot"></i></div>
                                    <div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-name">MT4 Expert Advisor (EA) with Auto Sizing</div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-desc">Use an EA with position sizing logic built in. The EA reads your account balance, applies your risk % setting, and calculates the lot size automatically before each trade — the gold standard for systematic traders.</div>
                                        <div class="br-platform-tags">
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">Automated</span>
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">System Trading</span>
                                            <span class="br-platform-tag">MQL4</span>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 6 — MT5 *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s6">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">6. Position Size Calculator for MT5</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">MetaTrader 5 improves on MT4 with a built-in <strong>Trade Calculator</strong> accessible from the toolbar (View → Toolbars → Trade levels). MT5 also supports more complex order types and a more powerful MQL5 scripting language for custom position size indicators.</p>
                            <p class="br-p">The same three approaches from MT4 apply on MT5 — indicator overlay, manual ticket entry, or EA automation — but the MQL5 Market has significantly more advanced tools including multi-symbol calculators, risk dashboards, and risk:reward line tools that auto-calculate lot size when you drag stop-loss and take-profit lines.</p>
                            <div class="br-callout">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-star me-2" style="color:#d97706;"></i>
                                <strong>MT5 advantage:</strong> MT5's built-in <em>Strategy Tester</em> lets you backtest your position sizing logic with real historical data — something MT4 cannot do as accurately. If you use automated sizing, MT5 is the better platform for validation.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 7 — Pip Value Table *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s7">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">7. Pip Value by Currency Pair (USD Account)</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Pip value varies by pair and changes when USD is not the quote currency. Below are standard pip values for a USD-denominated account.</p>
                            <table class="br-facts-table">
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Currency Pair</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Micro Lot (0.01)</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Mini Lot (0.10)</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Standard Lot (1.00)</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr><td><strong>EUR/USD</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.10</td><td style="text-align:center;">$1.00</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#16a34a;">$10.00</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>GBP/USD</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.10</td><td style="text-align:center;">$1.00</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#16a34a;">$10.00</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>USD/JPY</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.07</td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.70</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#2563eb;">~$7.00</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>AUD/USD</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.10</td><td style="text-align:center;">$1.00</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#16a34a;">$10.00</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>USD/CAD</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.07</td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.73</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#2563eb;">~$7.30</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>GBP/JPY</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.065</td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.65</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#d97706;">~$6.50</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>EUR/JPY</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.065</td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.65</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#d97706;">~$6.50</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>EUR/GBP</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">~$0.126</td><td style="text-align:center;">~$1.26</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#059669;">~$12.60</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>XAU/USD (Gold)</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.01</td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.10</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#d97706;">$1.00 / pip</td></tr>
                                    <tr><td><strong>US30 (Dow Jones)</strong></td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.01</td><td style="text-align:center;">$0.10</td><td style="text-align:center;font-weight:700;color:#7c3aed;">$1.00 / point</td></tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <p class="br-p" style="font-size:13px;color:#64748b;margin-top:-20px;">Values for JPY, CAD, and cross pairs fluctuate with exchange rates. Always verify with your broker's trade calculator before entering large positions.</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 8 — Gold *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s8">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">8. Position Sizing for Gold (XAU/USD)</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Gold is one of the most popular instruments for retail traders, but position sizing differs from standard Forex pairs because Gold moves in <strong>dollars per ounce</strong> rather than in pips. One standard lot of XAU/USD = 100 oz.</p>

                            <div class="psc-formula" style="margin-bottom:20px;">
                                <div class="psc-formula-title" style="color:#d97706;">Gold Position Size Formula</div>
                                <div class="psc-formula-eq">
                                    <span class="psc-formula-result">Position Size (lots)</span>
                                    <span class="psc-formula-equals">=</span>
                                    <span class="psc-formula-fraction">
                                        <span class="psc-formula-num">Account Risk ($)</span>
                                        <span class="psc-formula-bar"></span>
                                        <span class="psc-formula-den">Stop Loss ($/oz) &times; 100 oz</span>
                                    </span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <p class="br-p">Example: Risk $200 with a $10/oz stop-loss → Position Size = $200 ÷ ($10 × 100) = $200 ÷ $1,000 = <strong>0.20 lots</strong>. Because Gold is highly volatile, most brokers offer Gold in micro lots (0.01).</p>
                            <div class="br-callout">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation me-2" style="color:#d97706;"></i>
                                <strong>Gold warning:</strong> Gold moves 10–30× faster than EUR/USD in dollar terms during news events. Use wider stops ($8–$15/oz) and smaller lot sizes. Never apply the same pip-distance formula you use for Forex pairs.
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 9 — Common Mistakes *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s9">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">9. Common Position Sizing Mistakes</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Even traders who understand the formula regularly fall into these traps. Recognising them is the first step to avoiding them.</p>
                            <div class="psc-mistake-grid">
                                <div class="psc-mistake-card">
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i></div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-title">Risking More on "High-Confidence" Trades</div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-body">No setup has 100% probability. Doubling your risk on a "sure thing" is how accounts get blown. Keep risk consistent at 1–2% regardless of conviction.</div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="psc-mistake-card">
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i></div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-title">Using a Fixed Lot Size Always</div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-body">Trading 0.10 lots always means your risk varies with stop-loss distance. A 10-pip stop risks $10; a 50-pip stop risks $50. Standardise risk, not lot size.</div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="psc-mistake-card">
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i></div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-title">Setting Stop-Loss After Calculating Lot Size</div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-body">Always decide your stop-loss first based on market structure, then calculate lot size. Moving the stop to fit a desired lot size is backwards and dangerous.</div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="psc-mistake-card">
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i></div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-title">Ignoring Pip Value on Cross Pairs</div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-body">EUR/USD pip value is exactly $10/lot, but GBP/JPY pip value changes daily. Using $10 for all pairs will systematically over- or under-size your positions.</div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="psc-mistake-card">
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i></div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-title">Forgetting to Include Spread in Risk</div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-body">If your broker charges a 1.5-pip spread, your real stop-loss is wider. Factor in spread and commission on ECN accounts for accurate risk calculation.</div>
                                </div>
                                <div class="psc-mistake-card">
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-xmark"></i></div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-title">Opening Multiple Correlated Positions</div>
                                    <div class="psc-mistake-body">Holding EUR/USD long and GBP/USD long simultaneously doubles your USD exposure. Treat correlated pairs as a combined position when calculating total portfolio risk.</div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 10 — Fixed Risk vs Fixed Lot *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s10">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">10. Fixed Risk % vs Fixed Lot Sizing</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">There are two main approaches to position sizing in Forex. Understanding the difference explains why professional traders almost universally choose fixed-risk percentage sizing.</p>
                            <table class="br-facts-table">
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Criteria</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Fixed Lot Sizing</th>
                                        <th style="text-align:center;">Fixed Risk % Sizing</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Risk per trade</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">Varies with stop-loss</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;font-weight:700;">Constant % of account</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Lot size</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">Always the same</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;font-weight:700;">Adjusts with balance &amp; SL</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Account growth</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#ef4444;">Does not scale</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;font-weight:700;">Scales automatically</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Drawdown impact</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#ef4444;">Proportionally larger after losses</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;font-weight:700;">Smaller as balance decreases</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Strategy evaluation</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#ef4444;">Difficult — risk varies</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;font-weight:700;">Accurate R-multiples</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Best suited for</strong></td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;">Very short scalping only</td>
                                        <td style="text-align:center;color:#16a34a;font-weight:700;">All styles &amp; timeframes</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

                        @* Section 11 — Cashback *@
                        <div class="br-section" id="s11">
                            <h2 class="br-h2">11. How Cashback Reduces Your Effective Risk</h2>
                            <p class="br-p">Forex cashback rebates — paid per lot traded — directly reduce the net cost of every trade. This means your effective risk per trade is lower than the nominal amount, improving your mathematical edge over time.</p>
                            <div class="br-highlights">
                                <div class="br-highlights-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-dollar-to-slot me-2"></i>Cashback Reduces Effective Risk — Example</div>
                                <ul style="margin:0;padding:0 0 0 18px;line-height:2.2;font-size:14px;">
                                    <li>Trading <strong>0.50 lots EUR/USD</strong> with a $100 nominal risk</li>
                                    <li>Cashback rebate received: <strong>$3.75 (0.50 lots × $7.50/lot)</strong></li>
                                    <li>Effective risk after cashback: <strong>$100 − $3.75 = $96.25</strong></li>
                                    <li>Effective risk percentage: <strong>0.9625%</strong> instead of 1.00%</li>
                                    <li>Over 100 trades: <strong>$375 returned</strong> — meaningful P&amp;L improvement</li>
                                    <li>Cashback also lowers the break-even win rate on each trade</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <p class="br-p">The impact compounds with volume. Scalpers trading 10–30 lots per day see cashback become a major P&amp;L factor — often equivalent to recovering the full spread on every trade.</p>
                            <a asp-controller="Resources" asp-action="BestCashbackBrokers" class="br-cta-btn-primary" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-top:8px;">
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-trophy"></i> View Best Cashback Brokers 2025
                            </a>
                        </div>

                        <div class="br-divider"></div>

